00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.608
(narrator) Audio-described version: before each section, the picture pauses while I describe it.

2
00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:09.666
(narrator) The setting is a dark spy operations room with slow searchlights.

3
00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:14.162
(narrator) The intro: a huge Y2K, then a red stamp, the bug, as a beetle crawls in.

4
00:00:14.162 --> 00:00:22.467
(narrator) A vintage 1960s mainframe has two large tape reels and a small enlarged cutaway of its core memory: rings threaded on crossing wires.

5
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:28.837
(narrator) Memory was expensive, so split-flap digits for 1985 lose their first two digits and only 85 is stored.

6
00:00:28.837 --> 00:00:40.326
(narrator) A two-digit field rolls from 99 to 00, and a green-phosphor terminal shows the computer reading 19 plus 00 as 1900, and a desk calendar tears from December 31, 1999 to January 1, 1900.

7
00:00:40.326 --> 00:00:45.446
(narrator) Another terminal file computes an age as 00 minus 85, which is minus 85 years old.

8
00:00:45.446 --> 00:00:55.625
(narrator) Twelve case files, from payroll to grades, all carry the same red YY secret tag and turn red; an intercept console searches for YY and finds it in file after file.

9
00:00:55.625 --> 00:01:06.927
(narrator) A mission clock turns over to January 1, 2000, celebratory fireworks bloom around it, almost nothing breaks, and a classified expense sheet totals billions of dollars, stamped paid.

10
00:01:06.927 --> 00:01:13.858
(narrator) A case file gives the title, Every module keeps a secret, stamped top secret, while your hero sits at a laptop.

11
00:01:13.858 --> 00:01:18.627
[music]

12
00:01:18.627 --> 00:01:22.007
Y2K. No, not the aesthetic. The bug.

13
00:01:22.387 --> 00:01:25.767
Back in the sixties, memory was expensive.

14
00:01:26.127 --> 00:01:28.707
So programmers saved space:

15
00:01:28.707 --> 00:01:31.847
they stored the year with just two digits.

16
00:01:32.587 --> 00:01:35.347
1985? Just “85.”

17
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:39.647
Then comes the year 2000: “double-zero.”

18
00:01:39.747 --> 00:01:42.767
And the computer reads it as… 1900.

19
00:01:42.907 --> 00:01:46.847
Born in ’85? Zero minus 85:

20
00:01:47.027 --> 00:01:49.387
you’re negative 85 years old.

21
00:01:49.387 --> 00:01:53.747
Nobody thought this code would still run in 2000.

22
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:55.407
It did.

23
00:01:55.947 --> 00:01:59.007
And the two-digit year wasn’t hidden in one module.

24
00:01:59.007 --> 00:02:02.187
It was copied into every calculation.

25
00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:04.927
So engineers spent years hunting down

26
00:02:04.927 --> 00:02:07.087
every line that touched a year.

27
00:02:07.087 --> 00:02:10.307
Midnight came… and almost nothing broke,

28
00:02:10.307 --> 00:02:12.547
because they fixed it in time.

29
00:02:12.547 --> 00:02:16.227
The bill? Billions.

30
00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:20.607
Lesson one: every module keeps a secret.

31
00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:33.967
[music]

32
00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:35.348
(narrator) Verse 1, why it matters.

33
00:02:35.348 --> 00:02:44.612
(narrator) A vector portrait recreates Margaret Hamilton’s 1969 pose beside the bound Apollo listings: long hair, round glasses, patterned dress, and one hand on the stack.

34
00:02:44.612 --> 00:02:49.963
(narrator) Her record credits her as lead software engineer for Apollo’s onboard flight software at MIT.

35
00:02:49.963 --> 00:02:56.983
(narrator) Apollo 11, 1969: about 80 thousand lines of code across the command and lunar modules, excluding comments and blank lines.

36
00:02:56.983 --> 00:03:01.529
(narrator) Modern car, 2021: about 100 million lines across the vehicle, a Bosch estimate.

37
00:03:01.529 --> 00:03:05.211
(narrator) The counts use different scopes, not a precise complexity ratio.

38
00:03:05.211 --> 00:03:11.943
(narrator) A head opens like a hinged lid; three case files fit, then more cram in and papers burst out: no one can hold it all.

39
00:03:11.943 --> 00:03:18.676
(narrator) On a detective’s corkboard, red string joins every pair of four pinned modules, 6 connections; eight modules make 28.

40
00:03:18.676 --> 00:03:29.896
(narrator) When everything depends on everything, the strings pull taut, the cards tremble, and the whole board collapses into one Big Ball of Mud that lands with a splatter and stays beside the next scene.

41
00:03:29.896 --> 00:03:35.650
(narrator) One changed file gets eleven more stamped hit, and stamping out one bug brings two more crawling in.

42
00:03:36.247 --> 00:03:38.767
♪ Apollo’s code, printed out, ♪

43
00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:40.967
♪ stood as tall as Margaret Hamilton. ♪

44
00:03:40.967 --> 00:03:44.367
♪ A modern car? A hundred million lines. ♪

45
00:03:44.367 --> 00:03:47.027
♪ We’re not a thousand times smarter than back then, ♪

46
00:03:47.027 --> 00:03:50.007
♪ so no one holds the whole thing in their head. ♪

47
00:03:50.007 --> 00:03:52.267
♪ Four modules? Six connections. ♪

48
00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:54.887
♪ Eight modules? Twenty-eight. ♪

49
00:03:54.887 --> 00:03:57.907
♪ Let every part depend on every other part, ♪

50
00:03:57.907 --> 00:04:00.727
♪ and you’ve built a Big Ball of Mud: ♪

51
00:04:00.727 --> 00:04:03.727
♪ change one thing, touch dozens of files; ♪

52
00:04:03.727 --> 00:04:08.447
♪ fix one bug, and two more appear. ♪

53
00:04:09.333 --> 00:04:11.491
(narrator) Pre-chorus: how to split a system.

54
00:04:11.491 --> 00:04:17.902
(narrator) A flowchart of steps rides a conveyor belt, read, process, print, and gets a red stamp: not by steps.

55
00:04:17.902 --> 00:04:25.900
(narrator) Then, in a searchlight, the title page of Parnas’s 1972 paper, On the Criteria To Be Used in Decomposing Systems into Modules.

56
00:04:26.527 --> 00:04:30.287
♪ So how do you split a system into modules? ♪

57
00:04:30.287 --> 00:04:33.087
♪ Not by the steps it runs: first this, then that. ♪

58
00:04:33.087 --> 00:04:37.987
♪ In 1972, David Parnas wrote: ♪

59
00:04:37.987 --> 00:04:39.500
[music]

60
00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:48.134
(narrator) The chorus: a teleprinter sheet decrypts Parnas’s sentence line by line, with difficult design decisions marked gold and likely to change marked cyan.

61
00:04:48.134 --> 00:04:57.805
(narrator) Three case files, Dates, PaymentGateway, and UserRepository, each get a sealed top secret envelope, red laser walls switch on between them, and a decrypted stamp lands.

62
00:04:57.805 --> 00:05:06.785
(narrator) Then one module, Dates, is a steel vault: its brass nameplate, the interface, reads year of date; its X-ray window shows the secret, how the year is stored.

63
00:05:06.785 --> 00:05:15.650
(narrator) The dial spins and the secret changes from 85 to 1985, the nameplate stays the same, the callers stay unchanged, and a stamp says only one module changed.

64
00:05:16.233 --> 00:05:18.693
♪ We propose that one begins ♪

65
00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:21.693
♪ with a list of difficult design decisions ♪

66
00:05:21.693 --> 00:05:24.533
♪ or design decisions which are likely to change. ♪

67
00:05:24.533 --> 00:05:26.733
♪ Each module is then designed ♪

68
00:05:26.733 --> 00:05:29.693
♪ to hide such a decision from the others. ♪

69
00:05:29.693 --> 00:05:31.693
♪ (From the others!) Every module keeps a secret. ♪

70
00:05:31.693 --> 00:05:33.133
♪ Show the what, and hide the how. ♪

71
00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:34.553
♪ Change the how, but keep the what — ♪

72
00:05:34.553 --> 00:05:38.800
♪ (but keep the what!) You change one… one module. (Only one!) ♪

73
00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.551
(narrator) Verse 2, the PayPal tangle.

74
00:05:40.551 --> 00:05:52.805
(narrator) Your hero builds a store with three service case files, OrderService, RefundService, and WalletService; pink couriers carry envelopes from each straight to the PayPal SDK, and each holds the same sealed PayPal decision envelope.

75
00:05:52.805 --> 00:05:57.696
(narrator) The chief says we’re switching to Stripe, and an intercept console finds PayPal everywhere.

76
00:05:57.696 --> 00:06:04.576
(narrator) Then the tangle disappears: a PaymentGateway credential badge with charge and refund sits between the services and the provider.

77
00:06:04.576 --> 00:06:10.219
(narrator) A trench-coat secret agent appears in a spotlight, Agent Payment Gateway, whose secret is which provider.

78
00:06:10.219 --> 00:06:13.390
(narrator) Only a PayPalGateway file below it holds the PayPal secret.

79
00:06:13.390 --> 00:06:17.367
(narrator) A StripeGateway plugs in beside it, and all three services stay unchanged.

80
00:06:17.973 --> 00:06:20.913
♪ Your team builds a store: checkout, refunds, a wallet. ♪

81
00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.793
♪ All three services call PayPal directly. ♪

82
00:06:23.793 --> 00:06:26.573
♪ Three services, and all three know the secret. ♪

83
00:06:26.573 --> 00:06:29.373
♪ Then the boss walks in: “We’re switching to Stripe!” ♪

84
00:06:29.373 --> 00:06:31.973
♪ You search the code for PayPal… (It’s everywhere!) ♪

85
00:06:31.973 --> 00:06:34.553
♪ So hide it: one interface, Payment Gateway. ♪

86
00:06:34.553 --> 00:06:37.433
♪ Charge and refund. No PayPal in sight. ♪

87
00:06:37.433 --> 00:06:40.653
♪ (The name’s Gateway. Payment Gateway.) ♪

88
00:06:40.813 --> 00:06:43.993
♪ Only PayPal Gateway knows that PayPal exists. ♪

89
00:06:43.993 --> 00:06:46.833
♪ Stripe? Write a Stripe Gateway and plug it in: ♪

90
00:06:46.833 --> 00:06:50.573
♪ the three services don’t change at all. ♪

91
00:06:50.573 --> 00:06:52.267
[music]

92
00:06:52.267 --> 00:06:56.282
(narrator) Pre-chorus 2, the payoff Parnas promised: three clipboard briefings.

93
00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:59.590
(narrator) Flexibility, change one module and leave the rest alone.

94
00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.306
(narrator) Comprehensibility, study one module at a time.

95
00:07:02.306 --> 00:07:05.790
(narrator) Parallel work, teams side by side with little need to talk.

96
00:07:06.387 --> 00:07:08.907
♪ That’s the payoff Parnas promised: ♪

97
00:07:08.907 --> 00:07:11.767
♪ change one module, leave the rest alone; ♪

98
00:07:11.767 --> 00:07:14.607
♪ study the system one module at a time; ♪

99
00:07:14.607 --> 00:07:17.967
♪ split the work, and teams build side by side ♪

100
00:07:17.967 --> 00:07:20.227
♪ with little need to talk. ♪

101
00:07:20.227 --> 00:07:22.000
[music]

102
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.543
(narrator) The chorus again: the decrypted sentence, the three case files with their sealed secrets and laser walls.

103
00:07:27.543 --> 00:07:36.571
(narrator) This time the vault is PaymentGateway: its nameplate, charge order and payment, stays the same while its secret changes from PayPal to Stripe, and only one module changes.

104
00:07:37.167 --> 00:07:39.307
♪ We propose that one begins ♪

105
00:07:39.307 --> 00:07:42.347
