132
00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:07.100
♪ Your charger can’t tell where the power’s from: ♪

133
00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:10.280
♪ sun at noon, or wind at midnight. ♪

134
00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:15.160
♪ The outlet’s the interface. The plant’s the secret. ♪

135
00:09:16.367 --> 00:09:18.153
(narrator) The bridge, private isn’t enough.

136
00:09:18.153 --> 00:09:21.185
(narrator) Your hero thinks every field is private, so it’s hidden.

137
00:09:21.185 --> 00:09:28.168
(narrator) A classified code file shows the private fields, but the public checkout method returns a PayPalCharge and takes a PayPalAccount.

138
00:09:28.168 --> 00:09:34.015
(narrator) Only these public types are outlined in red, and paper slips with those names slide out of the file: leaked.

139
00:09:34.015 --> 00:09:39.266
(narrator) Private fields get a stamp, not enough; hiding decisions lights up gold beside a sealed envelope.

140
00:09:39.266 --> 00:09:44.679
(narrator) Then a client application sends a request to a server, with a separate response arrow pointing back.

141
00:09:44.679 --> 00:09:49.389
(narrator) Their visible contract includes the protocol: stateless or stateful, callers must know.

142
00:09:49.389 --> 00:09:54.099
(narrator) The database choice, SQL or NoSQL, stays inside the server boundary as a hidden detail.

143
00:09:54.693 --> 00:09:58.533
♪ “I made every field private. So it’s hidden, right?” ♪

144
00:09:58.673 --> 00:10:01.893
♪ But checkout still returns a PayPal Charge: ♪

145
00:10:01.893 --> 00:10:04.953
♪ the secret’s in the signature, in plain sight. ♪

146
00:10:05.033 --> 00:10:07.233
♪ It’s not about hiding data. ♪

147
00:10:07.233 --> 00:10:10.473
♪ It’s about hiding decisions. (Decisions!) ♪

148
00:10:10.473 --> 00:10:13.093
♪ And don’t hide what callers need to know: ♪

149
00:10:13.093 --> 00:10:17.673
♪ stateless or stateful? That goes in the contract. ♪

150
00:10:17.673 --> 00:10:19.633
[music]

151
00:10:19.633 --> 00:10:25.224
(narrator) The debrief: a clipboard checklist, list the likely changes, pick one, count the modules it touches.

152
00:10:25.224 --> 00:10:29.584
(narrator) Two dependency graphs compare the same six modules for a new payment provider.

153
00:10:29.584 --> 00:10:32.044
(narrator) Arrows point to what each module depends on.

154
00:10:32.044 --> 00:10:39.088
(narrator) In the hidden design, five callers depend on Gateway, and only Gateway depends on the external provider: only Gateway changes.

155
00:10:39.088 --> 00:10:42.889
(narrator) In the leaky design, all six depend on the provider: all six change.

156
00:10:42.889 --> 00:10:46.467
(narrator) The system boundary keeps the provider outside the module count.

157
00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:51.040
♪ Not sure you hid it well? Run the change test: ♪

158
00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:53.260
♪ list the changes likely to come. ♪

159
00:10:53.260 --> 00:10:56.640
♪ Pick one. Count the modules that would change. ♪

160
00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:00.667
♪ Just one? (You’re good!) Dozens? (Redesign!) ♪

161
00:11:00.967 --> 00:11:09.560
(narrator) The pop quiz, an interrogation under a swinging lamp: seven cards, each with a question and an answer hidden under classified tape that slides away when the answer is called.

162
00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:14.760
♪ Pop quiz, agent! Y2K: what got copied everywhere? ♪

163
00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.060
♪ (crowd) The two-digit year! ♪

164
00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:17.400
♪ What should a module hide? ♪

165
00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:19.100
♪ (crowd) A decision likely to change! ♪

166
00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:21.980
♪ The interface shows the what… or the how? ♪

167
00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:22.580
♪ (crowd) The what! ♪

168
00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:24.960
♪ Every field is private. Is the decision hidden? ♪

169
00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:26.500
♪ (crowd) Not if the signature leaks it! ♪

170
00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:29.200
♪ Your server’s database: show it, or hide it? ♪

171
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:29.800
♪ (crowd) Hide it! ♪

172
00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.000
♪ Stateless or stateful, to a client? ♪

173
00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:33.000
♪ (crowd) Show it! ♪

174
00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:37.360
♪ Split your code by steps, or by decisions? ♪

175
00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:38.400
♪ (crowd) Decisions! ♪

176
00:11:39.533 --> 00:11:46.352
(narrator) The final chorus: the decrypted sentence again, and the three case files with their sealed secrets behind laser walls.

177
00:11:46.352 --> 00:11:53.748
(narrator) The vault is now UserRepository: its nameplate, find by email, stays the same while its secret changes from MongoDB to Supabase.

178
00:11:53.748 --> 00:11:56.349
